   "source": [
    "# List of the inconsistent Loyalty IDs in customers dataset\n",
    "duplicates_by_name = customers[customers.duplicated(subset=[\"Loyalty#\"], keep=False)].sort_values(by=[\"Loyalty#\"])\n",
    "duplicates_by_name[['Loyalty#', 'First Name', 'Last Name']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7d4260a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- That means that different customers have the same loyalty id. This is a problem because we cannot identify the customers uniquely and can not map their flights correctly. We check how many entries in flights correspond to these inconsistent loyalty ids.\n",
    "  "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "id": "0675c91d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Number of flight entries with inconsistent loyalty IDs: 11772\n",
      "Percentage of flights affected by inconsistent loyalty IDs: 1.93%\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Count the number of entries in flights corresponding to the inconsistent loyalty ids in total\n",
    "inconsistent_loyalty_ids = duplicates_by_name['Loyalty#'].unique()\n",
    "flights_inconsistent = flights[flights['Loyalty#'].isin(inconsistent_loyalty_ids)]\n",
    "flights_inconsistent_count = flights_inconsistent['Loyalty#'].value_counts()\n",
    "flights_inconsistent_count.sum()\n",
    "print(\"Number of flight entries with inconsistent loyalty IDs:\", flights_inconsistent_count.sum())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Percentage of flights affected by inconsistent loyalty ids\n",
    "total_flights = flights.shape[0]\n",
    "affected_percentage = (flights_inconsistent_count.sum() / total_flights) * 100\n",
    "print(f\"Percentage of flights affected by inconsistent loyalty IDs: {affected_percentage:.2f}%\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e6ea1950",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- One Major problem of these duplicated loyalty ids is, that we cannot link the customers to their flights. There is no reasonable way to differentiate between the two customers with the same loyalty id. So that the even changing the loyalty id of one customer would not help, because we still do not know which flights belong to which customer. We would create a new customer with a new loyalty id, but this customer would have no flights linked to him. So we would loose all the information about the flights of these customers and the other customer would have all the flights linked to him. This would distort the data and make it unusable for analysis.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- So for this reason we have to delete all customers with duplicated loyalty ids and all their flights. This is the only way to ensure that the remaining data is valid and can be used for analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "- We may lose some customers, but there is no other way to ensure the validity of the data. The percentage of affected customers is 1.93%, which is relatively low compared to the total number of customers, so the impact on the analysis should be minimal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "id": "000ade4a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Cleaned Customers shape: (16594, 20)\n",
      "Cleaned Flights shape: (596664, 10)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Clean the customers and flights datasets by removing entries with duplicated Loyalty IDs\n",
    "loyalty_ids_to_remove = duplicates_by_name['Loyalty#'].unique()\n",
    "customers = customers[~customers['Loyalty#'].isin(loyalty_ids_to_remove)]\n",
    "flights = flights[~flights['Loyalty#'].isin(loyalty_ids_to_remove)]\n",
    "print(\"Cleaned Customers shape:\", customers.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Cleaned Flights shape:\", flights.shape)"
   ]
  },
